                <a href='javascript:void(0)' class='expand-result' title='Collapse result'></a>
        </div> 
    </div> 
    </div> 
</li>
    <li class="odd list-item-expanded">
<div class="number">
    <span class="digits">23.</span>
</div>
<div class="metadata">
    <div class="info" >
        <div class="subinfo">
            <h3>
                <span class="title"><a target="_parent" href="/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/19043/index.do?q=%222019+SCC+65%22">Northern Regional Health Authority v. Horrocks</a></span>
                        - <span class="citation">2021 SCC 42</span>
                    - <span class="report-citation">[2021] 3 SCR 107</span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">2021-10-22</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Supreme Court Judgments</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    Applied: Weber v. Ontario Hydro, [1995] 2 S.C.R. 929; Canada (Minister of Citizenship and Immigration) v. Vavilov, <b>2019 SCC 65</b>; Agraira v. Canada (Public Safety and Emergency Preparedness), 2013 SCC 36, [2013] 2 S.C.R. 559; considered: St. Anne Nackawic Pulp &amp; Paper Co. v. Canadian Paper Workers Union, Local 219, [1986]&nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[7] Decisions concerning the jurisdictional lines between two or more administrative bodies must be correct (Canada (Minister of Citizenship and Immigration) v. Vavilov, <b>2019 SCC 65</b>, at para. 53). This standard safeguards the rule of law, which “requires courts to intervene where one administrative body has interpreted the 
                </p>
